I have completed the investigation. Here is my report.

## Root cause

`Scheme()` in `/echo/context.go` (lines 252–271) accepts **any non-empty** value from the forwarded headers and returns it verbatim:

```go
252  func (c *context) Scheme() string {
253      // Can't use `r.Request.URL.Scheme`
254      // See: https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/golang-nuts/pMUkBlQBDF0
255      if c.IsTLS() {
256          return "https"
257      }
258      if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProto); scheme != "" {
259          return scheme                 // <-- returns "invalid" verbatim, no fall-through
260      }
261      if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProtocol); scheme != "" {
262          return scheme                 // <-- same problem
263      }
264      if ssl := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedSsl); ssl == "on" {
265          return "https"
266      }
267      if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXUrlScheme); scheme != "" {
268          return scheme                 // <-- same problem
269      }
270      return "http"
271  }
```

The guard `scheme != ""` only skips *absent* headers. An invalid value (e.g. `X-Forwarded-Proto: invalid`) is non-empty, so line 259 returns it immediately and the chain never reaches `X-Forwarded-Protocol` (line 261) or the `"http"` fallback (line 270). That is exactly why the two expected subtests fail. `IsTLS()` (line 243) and the `X-Forwarded-Ssl == "on"` check (line 264) are already correct; there is **no `SetScheme`** in this version, and no `SchemeHTTP`/`SchemeHTTPS` constants exist (`echo.go` only defines the header-name constants at lines 202–206).

## Minimal fix (unified diff for `/echo/context.go`)

Only the three `!= ""` guards need to become validity checks against the two schemes the interface documents (`http`/`https`):

```diff
--- a/context.go
+++ b/context.go
@@ -255,16 +255,16 @@
 	if c.IsTLS() {
 		return "https"
 	}
-	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProto); scheme != "" {
+	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProto); scheme == "http" || scheme == "https" {
 		return scheme
 	}
-	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProtocol); scheme != "" {
+	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProtocol); scheme == "http" || scheme == "https" {
 		return scheme
 	}
 	if ssl := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedSsl); ssl == "on" {
 		return "https"
 	}
-	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXUrlScheme); scheme != "" {
+	if scheme := c.request.Header.Get(HeaderXUrlScheme); scheme == "http" || scheme == "https" {
 		return scheme
 	}
 	return "http"
```

This is consistent with the rest of the repo, which treats schemes as the exact lowercase strings `"http"`/`"https"` (e.g. `middleware/redirect.go` compares `scheme != "https"`; `middleware/secure.go` compares the header to `"https"`). It also keeps the existing `context_test.go` cases passing (`HeaderXForwardedProtocol: "http"` and `HeaderXUrlScheme: "https"` are valid).

## Verification (no edits made to `/echo`)

Per instructions I did **not** touch `/echo/context.go` (confirmed `git diff --stat -- context.go` is empty). I applied the fix in a scratch copy `/tmp/fixtest` and ran:
- A reconstruction of the hidden test with the two named subtests plus the existing cases → all pass, including `ignores_invalid_X-Forwarded-Proto_and_uses_X-Forwarded-Protocol` and `ignores_invalid_forwarded_headers_and_falls_back_to_http`.
- Existing `TestContext_Scheme` → pass.
- Full suite `go test ./...` → `ok github.com/labstack/echo/v4`, `ok github.com/labstack/echo/v4/middleware`.

## Do other tests depend on the buggy behavior?

No. Every test that exercises scheme headers uses valid values only:
- `context_test.go:762,770,778,786` — `X-Forwarded-Proto: https`, `X-Forwarded-Protocol: http`, `X-Forwarded-Ssl: on`, `X-Url-Scheme: https`.
- `middleware/redirect_test.go:23,38,53` and `middleware/secure_test.go:31,51,72,82` — all `X-Forwarded-Proto: https`.

`middleware/secure.go` reads the header directly (`req.Header.Get(HeaderXForwardedProto) == "https"`) rather than via `Scheme()`, and `middleware/proxy.go` only *sets* `X-Forwarded-Proto` from `c.Scheme()` when it is empty — neither is affected. So the fix is safe and breaks nothing.

Note: the only uncommitted change in the working tree is an unrelated pre-existing `middleware/proxy.go` nil-balancer guard (plus a stashed WIP); it is not part of this bug.

Assumption worth flagging: "valid" is taken as an exact, case-sensitive match of `"http"`/`"https"`, matching the repo's existing comparisons. If the hidden test expects case-insensitive acceptance, the guards would need `strings.EqualFold` instead.

